    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only runnable test class; the other two files are a base and a log helper).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec says `riskFlag` is true when **any** of three conditions hold. I mapped each to the codebase's existing concepts and added it as a read-side derived field on the owner response (which is generated from `openapi.yml`):\n\n1. **Owner is a possible duplicate** \u2192 the stored `possibleDuplicate` flag is true.\n2. **Email domain is disposable-adjacent** \u2192 a new notion, distinct from the hard disposable blocklist (those emails are rejected at create, so they never reach a response). I defined it as: the domain shares the *registrable base label* of a known disposable domain (e.g. `mailinator.net`, `inbox.tempmail.com`) without being exactly on the blocklist.\n3. **City is over its soft capacity** \u2192 the existing capacity-warning band (`capacityWarning`): at or above the warning threshold of 40, below the hard limit of 50.\n\n### Changes\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `readOnly` `riskFlag` boolean to the `Owner` schema (regenerates into `OwnerDto` at build time).\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `riskFlag()` (the single source of truth), `isDisposableAdjacentEmailDomain(...)`, and a private `registrableBase(...)` helper, following the existing static-helper + Javadoc style.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` mapping expression and a `formatRiskFlag(owner)` default method, matching the other derived-field mappings.\n\nThe provided test (`coreRiskFlagFalseNormally`) creates a plain unique owner \u2014 no possible-duplicate, no email, no capacity warning \u2014 and now correctly reads `riskFlag: false`.\n\nNote: with only the one false-case test provided and no spec text defining \"disposable-adjacent\" or \"soft capacity\", I chose the interpretations above to be internally consistent with the app's existing thresholds (40/50) and blocklist. If the intended definitions differ, point me at them and I'll adjust.",
